2. Create Snyk Token

In your Snyk account, create an API token.

Then in Jenkins:

	Manage Jenkins → Credentials → Global → Add Credentials

Choose:

	Kind: Secret text
	Secret: <SNYK_TOKEN>
	ID: snyk-token

Your Jenkinsfile can then access it securely.



3. Snyk Dependency/SCA Scan

	For a Python project:

	snyk test --file=requirements.txt --severity-threshold=high


This detects vulnerable third-party dependencies.

Example:

	Django
	FastAPI
	requests
	urllib3
	pydantic


4. Add Snyk to Jenkins Pipeline


	 stage('Snyk SCA Scan') {
            steps {
                withCredentials([
                    string(
                        credentialsId: 'snyk-token',
                        variable: 'SNYK_TOKEN'
                    )
                ]) {
                    sh '''
                        snyk test \
                          --file=requirements.txt \
                          --severity-threshold=high
                    '''
                }
            }
        }



 5. Snyk Container Scanning

After Docker build:

	docker build -t myapp:${BUILD_NUMBER} .

Run:

	snyk container test myapp:${BUILD_NUMBER}

To fail the Jenkins build for High/Critical vulnerabilities:

	snyk container test \
	    myapp:${BUILD_NUMBER} \
	    --severity-threshold=high


You can make the pipeline stricter:

	--severity-threshold=critical

or:

	--severity-threshold=high

Typical production policy:

LOW       → Allow
MEDIUM    → Allow / Review
HIGH      → Fail
CRITICAL  → Fail



6. Snyk IaC Scanning

	Snyk can also scan Terraform/Kubernetes configuration.

For Terraform:

	snyk iac test terraform/

For Kubernetes manifests:

	snyk iac test k8s/



